e 20k's wall down the C-file and along row 4 held up to the end.", "moments": [{"turn": 17, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol's D6 lost 16.2 points and took Black's lead from -11.3 to -27.5. The engine wanted B5. D6 added a stone to a Black chain that was already hemmed in by White, and the game stopped being close here."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "F3 lost another 19.4 points, from -19.9 to -39.3, where the engine's choice was C6. The stone landed on the third line under White's row of D4, E4 and F4, and the deficit grew past 39 points."}, {"turn": 24, "text": "The 20k's C4 lost 25.7 points and cut White's lead from 41.0 to 15.2. The engine's choice was H6. C4 does tie the C-file stones to the D4 row, and that wall bounds the left side for the rest of the game, but it was still a big gift."}, {"turn": 45, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol's H5 lost 25.2 points, taking Black from -13.0 to -38.2. The engine preferred J3. It was the biggest swing of Black's game, in a position that had been steady at around -13."}, {"turn": 46, "text": "The 20k replied with H3, which lost 28.3 points and moved the lead from -38.2 back to -9.9. The engine's choice was H2. This was the worst move of the 20k's game, and it gave back everything H5 had won."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Everything it ran only worked on the current position or on records: current-position-liberties.js (inspectLiberties) lists chains and liberties from the board text, the flood-fill in command 32 counts empty regions and borders, command 35 builds the SGF and checks placements against the final board, and the rest played the named moves and wrote notes. None of it generated, ranked or looked ahead at candidate moves.", "stop": false}}